virglrenderer: draws only fetch inside their buffers

What: before any GL work for a draw, vrend checks that every vertex and instance it
fetches lies inside the vertex buffers: non-indexed draws from first and count,
indexed draws from the indices themselves, read back from the GL buffer (64-bit
math, index bias and primitive restart included); instanced attributes from the
first instance, instance count and divisor. The index range is checked in 64 bit
(it wrapped at 32), the index size must be 1, 2 or 4 and the offset aligned.
Indirect draws read their commands (and draw count) from the GL buffers and are
drawn as checked direct draws, so the GPU never takes an unchecked count. A draw
that would leave its buffers is skipped and logged (three lines per context); the
context lives. glDrawRangeElements gets the real index range. Two upstream bugs on
the way: the legacy attribute path re-enabled attributes whose vertex buffer was
unbound (XOR toggle), so they fetched from the old buffer with the new draw's
range; and a failed glMapBufferRange for a stride-0 attribute passed NULL to
glVertexAttrib*fv (QEMU crash).
Why: Apple's GL has no robust buffer access; its GPU fetches what it is told. A
fetch past a buffer reads memory other contexts own or makes the GPU fault; a
fault resets the GPU and on 2026-10-04 that hung WindowServer and panicked macOS.
A guest must not be able to do that. Reading the indices back grows with the
index count: on an M4 Max (bench lock, median of 3) under 1 us per draw for
12 KB, 0.03 ms for 1 MB, 0.11 ms for 4 MB of indices (ADR 0017); mapping instead
would cost 95 us per draw.
Depends on: virgl-buffer-binding-checks.patch (vrend_buffer_range_ok).
Upstream: upstreamable hardening for hosts without robust buffer access; not sent.
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-gpu-ranges.c (each refused draw and the accepted edge
cases, on Apple's software renderer); Tests/virgl/fuzz-cmd-stream.sh with
gl-oracle.c (checks every GL draw call independently).
